// error
// #4688: the callee shapes that still reject a named argument with E0128 now
// that a class CONSTRUCTOR accepts one. Pinned because a reader who has just
// seen `Acct(fee = 5)` compile would expect all three to, and because
// widening any of them silently is exactly what this file exists to catch.
//
//  1. `len(x = s)` — a BUILTIN (SPEC §5.3). A predeclared function has no
//     declaration in the program at all, so `x` has nothing to resolve
//     against. Refused ahead of the user-type lookup on purpose: a user
//     `class len { init(...) }` warns E0048 and still loses to the builtin in
//     `lowerCall`, so resolving against that class's `init` would have the
//     checker judging one callee and the lowerer emitting another.
//  2. `i64(v = 1)` — a primitive CONVERSION (SPEC §12.9). One operand,
//     positionally; a conversion declares no parameter and so names none.
//  3. `Point(x = 1)` — a class with NO `init`. In call position that is the
//     same one-argument conversion rule as 2; the field-init syntax
//     `Point{ x: 1 }` (#3840) is the spelling that names fields, and it is
//     unaffected.

class Point {
  x: i64,
  y: i64,
}

fn main() {
  let s = "abc"
  print("${len(x = s)}\n")
  print("${i64(v = 1)}\n")
  let p = Point(x = 1)
  print("${p.x}\n")
}
